@Tutorial(time: 50) {
    @Intro(title: "Setting up the game area") {
        In this first part, we're going to set up the game area and register the
        player's input actions. Let's get started by importing the start assets
        and setting up the game scene.

        We've prepared a starter project with the 3D models and sounds we'll use for this
        tutorial. You can download the archive here:
        [Save the Axolotl: Snake Stomp assets](https://github.com/xogot-projects/Your-First-3D-Game-Project/releases/download/starters/3d_save_the_axolotl_snake_stomp.zip).
    }
    @Section(title: "Importing the start assets") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Once you've downloaded the archive in Safari, open the Files App and
            navigate to `Downloads`. Tap the `3d_save_the_axolotl_snake_stomp.zip`
            file to extract it.

            If your iPad supports multitasking, you can use
            **Stage Manager (if enabled)** to keep Xogot and the Files App
            side-by-side. 
            @Image(source: 01-01.unzip-project.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the Xogot Project Manager, tap the **Folder** button to import
                the project.

                @Image(source: 01-02.import-button.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the import popup, navigate to the freshly created
                directory `3d_save_the_axolotl_snake_stomp/`. 
                
                Tap *Open* to open the project in the editor.

                @Image(source: 01-03.browse-to-project-folder.png)
            }
            > A window notifying you that the project was generated by an older
            > engine version may appear.
            > If that prompt appears, tap *OK* to convert the project to your
            > current engine version.

            > Important: If the project didn't open automatically, open it from your project
            > list.

            @Step {
                The start project contains an icon and two folders: `art/` and `fonts/`.
                There, you will find the art assets and music we'll use in the game.

                @Image(source: 01-04.start-assets.png)
            }

            There are two 3D models, `player_Axolotl.glb` and `enemy_snake.glb`,
            some materials that belong to these models, and a music track. You'll
            also find a `resources/` folder containing a pre-made
            animation clip and a gradient texture we'll use later in the
            series.

            > Tip: The `.glb` files contain 3D scene data based on the open
            > source glTF 2.0 specification. They're a modern and powerful
            > alternative to a proprietary format like FBX, which Xogot also
            > supports. To produce these files, we designed the models in
            > [Blender 3D](https://www.blender.org/) and exported them to glTF.
            > We'll work with them in the next lessons.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Setting up the playable area") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We're going to create our main scene with a plain
            [Node](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode.html#class-node)
            as its root. Then we'll add a floor that prevents the characters from
            falling, and a directional light so the scene isn't all grey.

            To create static colliders like the floor, walls, or ceilings, you can
            use
            [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
            nodes. They require
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            child nodes to define the collision area.

            @Image(source: 01-05.static-body-collision-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the _Scene Tree_, tap the **Create New Node** button (the "+"
                icon), tap the *All* tab and tap on *Node*. Name the node `Main`.

                > Tip: An alternate method to rename the node is to long-press on
                > *Node* and choose *Rename*.

                @Image(source: 01-05a.create-main-node.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene as `main.tscn` by tapping **Save** in the
                **Document Menu**.
                
                A good habit is to create a directory for your scenes to stay organized. To do that press the **...** menu in the top right of the window, and select *Create Folder*. Name the folder `scenes`. Once generated tap the new folder to enter it and press *Save*.

                > Important: Saving early and often prevents accidental loss of
                > work if the app closes unexpectedly.

                @Image(source: 01-06.save-scene-dialog.png, alt: "Save the scene as main.tscn")
            }
            @Step {
                With `Main` selected, add a
                [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
                child node, then a
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
                child of the `StaticBody3D`. Rename the `StaticBody3D` to `Ground`.
                
                Your scene tree should look like this.

                @Image(source: 01-07.adding-static-body3d.png)
            }
            
            @Step {
                > Note: A warning sign next to the `CollisionShape3D` appears because
                > we haven't defined its shape yet. If you tap the warning icon, a
                > popup appears with more information.

                @Image(source: 01-08.collision-shape-warning.png)
            }

            @Step {
                Select the `CollisionShape3D` node. If the _Inspector_ is hidden,
                tap the _Inspector_ sidebar icon in the upper-right toolbar to show it. Then tap
                the **Pick Resource** field next to the *Shape* property and create a new
                *BoxShape3D*.

                > Tip: The box shape is perfect for flat ground and walls. Its
                > thickness makes it reliable to block even fast-moving objects.

                @Image(source: 01-09.create-box-shape3d.png)
            }
            A box's wireframe appears in the viewport with six orange dots. You
            can tap and drag these to edit the shape's extents interactively. We
            can also precisely set the size in the inspector.

            @Step {
                Tap on the
                [BoxShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d)
                to expand the resource. Set its *Size* to `60` on the X-axis, `2`
                for the Y-axis, and `60` for the Z-axis.

                @Image(source: 01-10.box-size-settings.png)
            }
            Collision shapes are invisible. We need to add a visual floor that goes
            along with it.

            @Step {
                Select the `Ground` node and add a
                [MeshInstance3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fmeshinstance3d.html#class-meshinstance3d)
                as its child.

                @Image(source: 01-11.mesh-instance3d-node.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, tap on the field next to *Mesh* and create a
                [BoxMesh](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxmesh.html#class-boxmesh)
                resource to create a visible box.

                @Image(source: 01-12.box-mesh-creation.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Once again, it's too small by default. Tap the box icon to expand
                the resource and set its *Size* to `60`, `2`, and `60`.

                @Image(source: 01-13.cube-resized-viewport.png)
            }
            You should see a wide grey slab that covers the grid and blue and red
            axes in the viewport.

            We're going to move the ground down so we can see the floor grid. To
            do this, the grid snapping feature can be used.

            @Step {
                Start by setting snapping by tapping the *Use Snap* button in the 3D Editor Toolbar. 
                Then move the `Ground` node using the Y-axis (the green arrow on the gizmo).

                > Note: If you can't see the 3D object manipulator like on the
                > image above, ensure *Move Mode* is active in the 3D Editor
                > Toolbar.

                @Image(source: 01-14.move-gizmo-y-axis.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `Ground` node and move it down `1` meter, in order to have a visible editor
                grid. A label in the bottom-left corner of the viewport tells you
                how much you're translating the node.

                > Important: Moving the *Ground* node down moves both children
                > along with it. Ensure you move the *Ground* node, **not** the
                > *MeshInstance3D* or the *CollisionShape3D*.

                Ultimately, `Ground`'s transform.position.y should be -1

                @Image(source: 01-15.ground-moved-down-1-meter.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's add a directional light so our scene isn't all grey. Select
                the `Main` node and add a child node
                [DirectionalLight3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fdirectionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d).

                @Image(source: 01-16.create-directional-light3d.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the `DirectionalLight3D` up by tapping and dragging on the
                manipulator's green arrow, then switching to `Rotate Mode` tap and drag on the red arc to
                rotate it around the X-axis until the ground is lit.

                In the _Inspector_, turn on *Shadow* by tapping the checkbox.

                @Image(source: 01-17.turn-on-shadows.png)
            }
            @Step {
                At this point, your project should look like this:

                @Image(source: 01-18.project-with-light.png)
            }

            That's our starting point. In the next part, we will build the
            player scene and import the player model.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating input actions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With the game area ready, let's set up the input actions we'll use
            to move the character. In Xogot, while we could write all the key
            bindings in code, there's a powerful system that allows you to
            assign a label to a set of keys and buttons. This simplifies our
            scripts and makes them more readable.

            This system is the Input Map.

            > Tip: Once you set up an action here, you can reuse it across
            > scripts with `Input.is_action_pressed("your_action_name")`. You
            > can also bind multiple keys and gamepad buttons to a single
            > action.

            @Image(source: 01-19.input-map-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                To access the Input Map editor, tap the **Menu** toolbar button in
                the upper-right corner of Xogot and choose **Settings**. In the
                Settings dialog, tap the **Dropdown Menu** next to **General** and select
                **Input Map**.

                @Image(source: 01-20.project-settings-menu.png)
            }
            @Step {
                The **Input Map** panel allows you to add new actions on the left side. 
                These are the actions your game will respond to, and they are your labels. 
                In the right part, you can bind keys to these actions.

                > Tip: Xogot projects come with some predefined actions designed
                > for user interface design. These will become visible if you
                > enable the **Show Built-in Actions** toggle. We could use these
                > here, but instead we're defining our own to support gamepads.
                > Leave **Show Built-in Actions** disabled.

                @Image(source: 01-21.input-map-tab.png)
            }
            We're going to name our actions `move_left`, `move_right`,
            `move_forward`, `move_back`, and `jump`.

            @Step {
                To add an action, tap the **+** icon to the top of the panel and then type its name.

                @Image(source: 01-22.adding-action.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Create the following five actions: `move_left`, `move_right`,
                `move_forward`, `move_back`, and `jump`.

                @Image(source: 01-23.actions-list-empty.png)
            }
            @Step {
                To bind a key or button to an action, select the action you want to
                add to and then press the **Add Action Event** button to the
                right. Do this for `move_left`.

                We want to add the `A` key first by searching for it in the Search box
                and pressing **Create**.

                @Image(source: 01-24.bind-left-a-key.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Bind the `Left` Arrow key in the same manner by searching for `Left`,
                selecting the key and pressing **Create** (found under *Navigation*).
                @Image(source: 01-25.bind-left-arrow-key.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's now add support for a gamepad's left joystick. Tap the "**+**"
                button again and select the `Left`, `Joystick 0 Left` axis (Axis 0-) under
                **Joypad Axes**.

                > Note: If you want controllers to have different input actions,
                > you can use the Devices option in the All Devices **Dropdown Menu**. Device 0
                > corresponds to the first plugged gamepad, Device 1 to the
                > second, and so on. We're leaving this off so a single gamepad
                > configuration works for any plugged-in controller.
                > This is one way you could enable multiplayer support.

                @Image(source: 01-26.bind-joystick-axis.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Do the same for the other input actions. For example, bind the `right
                arrow`, `D`, and the left joystick's `0+ axis` to `move_right`.
                After binding all keys, your interface should look like this.

                @Image(source: 01-27.all-movement-inputs-mapped.png)
            }
            @Step {
                The final action to set up is the `jump` action. Bind this action
                to the `Space` key. Also bind the gamepad's `A`
                button, known to Xogot as `Bottom Action`, located under **Joypad Buttons**.

                Your jump input action should look like this.

                @Image(source: 01-28.jump-input-action.png)
            }
            That's all the actions we need for this game. Now let's add
            on-screen controls so you can play the game on your iPhone or iPad.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding on-screen controls") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            On iPhone and iPad, the player needs on-screen controls. Let's
            add a customizable on-screen joystick add-on that will work with
            the input actions we just created.

            The add-on we'll use is
            [*Virtual Joystick*](https://github.com/MarcoFazioRandom/Virtual-Joystick-Godot)
            by Marco Fazio. It's an open-source add-on that gives you a
            full touch thumbstick on screen and lets you wire it to any
            input action.

            > Note: Because this game is built for iOS, the on-screen joystick
            > is required to play without an external keyboard or gamepad. You
            > should still keep the keyboard and gamepad bindings we just set up
            > — they work alongside the joystick.

            > Tip: There are lots of other add-ons for Xogot, with more
            > being added all the time. Trying out the available add-ons
            > is a great way to learn Xogot.

            @Image(source: 01-29.virtual-joystick-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Tap on the menu to the left of the _Inspector_ icon and
                access the **Asset Library**.

                @Image(source: 01-30.asset-library-menu.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Find or search for **Virtual Joystick** and once
                selected, tap **Get** and then **Import**

                @Image(source: 01-31.asset-library-browser.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Exit the **Asset Library**. You should now have an
                `addons/` folder in your project. Inside you'll find the
                `virtual_joystick/` folder containing its scene.

                > Tip: You can also find example implementations inside
                > the add-on's `test/` folder if you'd like to dive
                > deeper.

                @Image(source: 01-32.addons-folder-structure.png)
            }
            Now we'll add the joystick to the `main.tscn` scene we created
            earlier. We'll set up a UI layer to host the joystick.

            @Step {
                With the `Main` node selected, add a
                [CanvasLayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_canvaslayer.html#class-canvaslayer)
                child node and rename it to `UI`.

                @Image(source: 01-33.rename-canvas-layer-ui.png)
            }
            @Step {
                With the new `UI` node selected, instantiate a child
                scene by tapping the _Chain_ icon and pick the Virtual Joystick scene:
                `virtual_joystick_scene.tscn`.

                Rename the instantiated node to `Virtual Joystick Left`.

                @Image(source: 01-34.instantiate-virtual-joystick.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the joystick node and in the _Inspector_ set the
                **Joystick Mode** to `Following` and the **Visibility
                Mode** to `When Touched`.

                `Following` means the joystick appears wherever the
                player first touches, so it doesn't get in the way of
                the rest of the UI.

                @Image(source: 01-35.joystick-mode-settings.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Still in the _Inspector_, change `action_left` through
                to `action_down` so they equal the names of the custom
                input actions we added in the Input Map: `move_left`,
                `move_right`, `move_forward`, and `move_back`.

                > Important: Make sure you keep track of capitalization
                > as these variable names are case-sensitive. They must
                > match the action names exactly.

                @Image(source: 01-36.joystick-action-bindings.png)
            }
            @Step {
                With the `Virtual Joystick Left` node still selected,
                open the anchor controls (1) and set the anchor mode to
                `Full Rect` (2).

                This makes the joystick follow the player's touch
                anywhere on the left half of the screen.

                @Image(source: 01-37.joystick-full-rect-anchors.png)
            }
            The joystick is now set up and will appear when you
            touch the screen. Because we set the **Visibility Mode** to
            `When Touched`, it won't be visible until you actually touch
            the screen, keeping the game view clean.

            > Tip: The add-on also exposes a `Virtual Joystick Right`
            > scene if you want to add a second stick for things like
            > camera control. The setup is the same.

            Now we need to enable the built-in Virtual Controller for
            additional buttons like jumping.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Enabling the Virtual Controller") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The Virtual Joystick handles movement, but we also need a button
            for jumping. Xogot includes a built-in Virtual Controller that
            provides on-screen buttons for iOS. Let's enable it and configure
            it to show only the jump button we need.

            @Image(source: 01-38.virtual-controller-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Tap the **Menu** toolbar button in the upper-right corner
                of Xogot and choose **Settings** to open the Project Settings
                dialog.

                In the Settings dialog, from the General menu, scroll down to the **Input Devices** section.

                @Image(source: 01-20.scroll-project-settings-menu.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the **Input Devices** section, find the **Virtual Controller**
                settings. Turn **ON** the **Enable Controller** switch.

                This enables the built-in virtual controller for iOS devices.

                @Image(source: 01-39.enable-virtual-controller.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Still in the **Virtual Controller** settings, make sure all
                other switches are turned **OFF** except for **Enable Button A**.

                We only need Button A for jumping. The Virtual Joystick
                we added earlier will handle all the movement controls.

                > Note: Button A will automatically trigger the `jump` input
                > action we created earlier, since we mapped it to the gamepad's
                > "Bottom Action" button.

                @Image(source: 01-40.enable-button-a-only.png)
            }
            Finally we need to disable one setting toggle to make the Joystick work well.
            @Step {
                In the **Pointing** page, set **Emulate Touch From Mouse** to **ON** and **Emulate Mouse From Touch** to **OFF**
                @Image(source: 01.41.emulate-touch-toggle.png)
            }
            Perfect! Later when you run the game on an iPhone or iPad, you'll
            see both the Virtual Joystick for movement and an on-screen
            Button A for jumping.

            That's all the setup we need for now. In the next part,
            we'll build the player scene and import the player model.
        }
    }
}
